course: dictionary
lesson: 7286
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. «I'd give that clock away ten times to have one more pint with him.» Ask yourself: what would fit here? You would not give away a clock for a pint of milk, so the friend is probably sharing a drink, not measuring anything.
explain: The sentence is about being with someone, so pint here means a glass of beer, as in «going for a pint». Try the meaning in the sentence: «one more glass of beer with him» still makes sense, so you have chosen well.
explain: Pint can also be a measure, as in «a pint of milk», or describe something tiny, as in «pint-sized». Check each meaning against the words around it before you decide.
fact: Pint is a noun: a pint, pints.
fact: A pint is a unit for measuring liquids such as milk or beer.
fact: In the UK, an imperial pint is about 568 millilitres; in the US, a pint is about 473 millilitres.
fact: 8 pints make a gallon, and 2 pints make a quart.
fact: «Going for a pint» is an informal UK way of saying you are going for a glass of beer, and «pint-sized» means very small.
exercise: How many pints make a gallon? | *8 | 16 | 10 | 4
exercise: «I'd give that clock away ten times to have one more pint with him.» What does pint most likely mean here? | A unit of time | A bottle of medicine | *A glass of beer, shared with a friend | A tiny clock
exercise: Something that is «pint-sized» is? | Very loud | Very old | Very large | *Very small
test: In the UK, a full pint is about 568 millilitres. About how much is half a pint? | About 142 millilitres | About 1 litre | *About 284 millilitres | About 568 millilitres
test: Which is smaller: a UK pint or a US pint? | They are both one litre | The UK pint | They are exactly the same | *The US pint
exam: Which sentence uses pint to mean a glass of beer? | «The pint-sized puppy ran in.» | *«Let's go for a pint after work.» | «The bottle holds one pint of water.» | «Please buy a pint of milk.»
